# Line ending policy.
#
# Windows Git ships with core.autocrlf = true at system level
# (C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig). With no .gitattributes the working tree
# is checked out CRLF, which breaks shebangs when the same checkout is driven
# from WSL and makes WSL-side git report every tracked file as modified. The
# policy belongs to the repository, not to a developer's machine config.

# Store every text file with LF in the object store. Git's own text/binary
# detection decides what counts as text; the rules below cover the cases where
# that detection, or the platform-native checkout, must not be trusted.
* text=auto

# Executed through a shebang. A CR at the end of the shebang line becomes part
# of the interpreter name (/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r'), so these must be LF in the
# working tree on every platform, not only in the index. Every tracked *.sh and
# *.py is covered by the two rules below, so no count needs maintaining here.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Binary. Both are correctly detected by text=auto today, but map.pgm is a
# regenerated artifact (sim/scenarios/tools/make_map.py) whose leading bytes are
# not guaranteed to contain the NUL the heuristic looks for, and a misdetected
# binary is silently corrupted by normalization rather than merely inconvenient.
*.pgm -text
*.gz -text

# Media in assets/ and the committed watch-table captures. Detection is correct
# today, but the rule costs one line and a misdetected binary is corruption.
*.png -text
*.gif -text
*.mp4 -text

# slam_toolbox's serialised pose graph. Neither extension is covered by the
# rules above and both currently rely on git's heuristic, which only scans the
# first 8000 bytes for a NUL — the same gap that made map.pgm a stated rule.
# The pose graph exists so mapping can resume rather than restart, so silent
# corruption would be discovered only at the run that needed it.
*.posegraph -text
# The .data half was path-scoped to sim/maps/ and F3's map lives in
# m5_ver3/maps/, where it fell back on the heuristic the rule above
# exists to distrust. It is scoped by EXTENSION now, for the reason the
# posegraph rule beside it gives: the two files are one artifact and a
# rule that covers one of them is a rule that covers neither.
*.data -text

# A HASH-BOUND ARTIFACT IS NOT TEXT TO BE TIDIED. The occupancy grid and
# the transform fitted to it are bound by md5: registration.yaml carries
# `map_md5` and `map_yaml_md5`, and `map_register.load_registration`
# refuses a transform whose grid has changed underneath it. Commit
# 9c52aa6 ran end-of-file-fixer across the tree and added the missing
# final newline to warehouse_v3.yaml - 136 bytes to 137 - which changed
# its md5 from a3c76218 to 1a35bb79 and made `m5v3.sh start` REFUSE at
# "the map on disk is the map the registration was fitted to". Pure
# formatting is not pure when the bytes are the artifact. Marking these
# binary stops Git normalising them and stops a text hook seeing them.
m5_ver3/maps/** -text
